---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/adityaokke/programmersbible";
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What Programmer's Bible knows about you: nothing if you only read, and, if you choose to sign in with Google, your name, email address and reading progress.">
  <div class="narrow">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="small muted">Last updated: 14 November 2026</p>

    <p>
      <strong>In short:</strong> you can read everything without an account. If you do not sign in, this site stores
      nothing about you: it sets no cookies of its own, stores nothing in your browser and shows no ads. If you choose
      to sign in with Google, the site keeps a small account so it can remember what you have read. You can download
      that data or delete it at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>What the site collects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>If you do not sign in: nothing about you.</strong> No account, no cookies of the site's own, nothing
        stored in your browser.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Visit counts.</strong> The site uses{" "}
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a> to count visits: which pages are
        read and how often, from which countries, and which websites sent readers here. Cloudflare says this tool does
        not use cookies or local storage and does not "fingerprint" visitors. We only see totals, not individual people.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The address of a map view.</strong> When you move around the <a href="/map">map</a>, the place you are
        looking at is written into the page address, so the Back button works. This stays in your own browser history.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you sign in</h2>
    <p>
      Signing in is optional. You sign in with your Google account; the site has no passwords. Your account holds:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>From your Google account:</strong> your name, your email address, the address of your profile picture
        (the picture itself stays on Google's servers), whether Google says your email address is verified, and
        Google's number for your account, so the site recognizes you the next time. The site asks Google for nothing
        else, and it does not keep the keys ("tokens") Google gives it, so it cannot do anything in your Google
        account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your reading progress:</strong> which topics you opened, which ones you marked as read, the last
        section of each topic you reached, and when.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your sign-ins:</strong> when you signed in and when that sign-in ends. The site does not keep your IP
        address or the name of your browser with it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>This data is used only to show you your own progress. It is not used for ads, not sold and not shared.</p>

    <h2>Cookies, only when you sign in</h2>
    <p>The site's cookies are its own and are used for signing in only. Readers who do not sign in get none of them.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The session cookie</strong> (its name ends in <code>better-auth.session_token</code>) keeps you signed
        in. It lasts until you sign out, or until about 30 days after you last used the site. Scripts on the page
        cannot read it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A sign-in check</strong> (its name ends in <code>better-auth.state</code>) lasts 5 minutes while you sign
        in. It makes sure the answer that comes back from Google belongs to the sign-in you started. The database keeps a
        copy of that check, which says nothing about who you are; it expires after 10 minutes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A "signed in" note</strong> (<code>pb_signed_in</code>) holds only the number 1. It tells the site's
        pages that someone is signed in on this browser, so they ask for your progress; without it, pages ask for
        nothing.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Download or delete your data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Download:</strong> on <a href="/me">your account page</a>, <strong>Download my data</strong> gives you
        everything the site keeps about you, as a file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delete:</strong> on the same page, <strong>Delete my account</strong> deletes your account, your
        sign-ins and your reading progress from the site's database at once. For safety, this needs a sign-in from the
        last day; the page asks you to sign in again if needed. The database's own backups, kept by Cloudflare so
        mistakes can be undone, are erased after at most 30 days.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign out</strong> on the same page ends your sign-in on this browser.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> delivers the site and stores the accounts database. Like any web host, it handles
        technical data such as your IP address to send you the pages and to protect the site from attacks. See{" "}
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google</strong>: if you sign in, you do so on Google's own page, so Google knows you signed in to this
        site. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>. You can remove the site's
        access in your Google account, under{" "}
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">third-party apps and services</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub</strong>: when you use <strong>Report a problem</strong>, you leave this site and write your
        report on GitHub, with your GitHub account. Reports are public. See{" "}
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other websites</strong>: topics link to their sources and to "Try it" pages on other websites. Those
        websites have their own privacy rules.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      On 14 November 2026 the site added optional accounts (sign in with Google) and reading progress, described
      above. If the site changes what it stores, this page will say so first, with the date it changed. The history of
      this page is public on <a href={`${REPO}/commits/main/src/pages/privacy.astro`}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>Open an issue on <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>GitHub</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
